---
/**
 * Scene 0–1: a month arrives, and the first piece of work gets done to it.
 *
 * WHAT THIS SCENE ARGUES, AND WHY IT IS BUILT THIS WAY.
 *
 * The order of events is the argument. A month does not arrive sorted; it
 * arrives as a heap, and somebody sorts it. So the paper goes:
 *
 *   edges (illegible)  →  a heap in the middle  →  five named documents
 *
 * and not the other way round. An earlier version had the sheets tidy
 * themselves into a grid and then collapse into a pile, which is the same
 * motion played backwards: it looked like work being undone, it proved nothing,
 * and the number at the end of it was a number the visitor had no reason to
 * care about. Chaos → order, in that direction, is the whole thesis of the
 * site; every scene has to run that way.
 *
 * Each beat carries a heading and a line of copy, in the ordinary place a
 * heading goes — across the top, before the thing it describes. The animation
 * illustrates the sentence. It does not replace it. A pinned section that moves
 * paper in silence is a screensaver, and a visitor who has to reverse-engineer
 * what he just watched has already gone.
 *
 * The $84,200 is the PROBLEM, not the payoff: money that moved through a
 * business with not one line of it checked. It sets up the work rather than
 * concluding it.
 *
 * COMPOSITION AT REST
 *
 * The middle of the screen is a cleared patch of desk — the space someone
 * pushed the paper aside to make. Everything that converts lives in the
 * clearing; the paper is pushed to the edges of the frame, cropped and
 * illegible. Two consequences: the CTA cannot be covered, because the centre is
 * empty by construction, and there is no seam, because a clearing in a covered
 * desk is one image rather than two columns pretending to be one.
 *
 * Density is deliberately uneven — heavy down the right and along the bottom,
 * nothing in the top-left. An even border of paper reads as a certificate.
 *
 * Every sheet's life is nine numbers: where it rests (--cx/--cy/--crot), where
 * it heaps (--mx/--my/--mrot/--ms), where it is laid out (--tx/--ty/--trot/--ts)
 * and when it moves (--d1/--d2). One expression drives all nine.
 *
 * Layers per document, outermost first:
 *   .slot   the journey — position, rotation, scale, shadow. Driven by --p.
 *   .drift  the wind, damped to nothing as the sheet joins the order.
 *   .float  ambient breathing, same damping, and the sheet's one font size.
 */
import DocFace from './paper/DocFace.astro';
import BeatBand from './BeatBand.astro';
import { firm } from '../config/firm';
import { hero, beats } from '../config/content';
import { docById, clutter } from '../config/story';
import { em } from '../lib/format';
import { RUN } from '../config/scenes';
import { href } from '../lib/url';

/**
 * Sorting order, which is also reading order in the laid-out grid: the eye
 * travels left to right, top to bottom, and finishes on the biggest number.
 * z-index rises the same way, so a sheet can only ever be covered on the side
 * you have already read.
 */
const ORDER = ['operating', 'tax', 'payroll', 'bank', 'invoices'];
const sheets = ORDER.map((id, i) => ({ doc: docById[id]!, z: 10 + i * 2 }));
const noise = clutter.map((doc, i) => ({ doc, z: 11 + i * 2 }));
---

<section class="opening" data-scene="opening" id="top" style={`--run:${RUN.opening}`}>
  <div class="opening__pin">
    <!--
      Pencil, on the desk UNDER the paper.

      EVERY MARK RUNS FROM THE CENTRE OF ONE SHEET TO THE CENTRE OF ANOTHER, and
      that rule is the whole reason this finally works. Three earlier versions
      ended a line near the EDGE of the sheet it pointed at — at a hand-picked
      percentage of the frame — and every one of them came out with the mark
      stopping in mid-air short of the paper, or crossing over it. It could not
      have done anything else: where a sheet's edge falls depends on the
      viewport height, the letterhead, the heading band and the type size, and a
      number typed into a path knows none of those. The two could only agree at
      the one width they were tuned on.

      A sheet's CENTRE, though, is exactly the coordinate the sheet is
      positioned by — `--tx`, `--ty` below, the same numbers, in the same
      hundredths of the frame. So both ends of every mark are underneath paper
      by construction, at every size, and only the middle is ever seen. There is
      nothing left to tune and nothing left to break.

      Which sheet a mark points at is the argument. The three cost documents run
      into the two records the next scene checks against each other.

      TWO MORE MARKS USED TO RUN OFF THE BOTTOM EDGE, to be picked up by the next
      scene at the same x, on the theory that a line continuing past the fold is
      a reason to keep scrolling. It never read that way. What it actually
      produced was two hairlines hanging in empty desk above the next scene's
      documents, pointing at nothing, for the whole length of the join — and it
      cost two rounds of geometry to make them meet before it was obvious that
      meeting was not the problem. They are gone, and so is the pair that
      answered them below. The scene's own three marks fade out with it.

      NO vector-effect here, and that is deliberate. non-scaling-stroke measures
      the dash pattern in screen pixels, so `stroke-dasharray: 1` stops meaning
      "one dash the length of the path" and starts meaning "dashes one pixel
      long" — a dotted line, fully drawn, visible from the first screen, with
      the draw-on animation doing nothing but sliding the dots. Without it the
      dashes are in user units, pathLength normalises them to 1, and the line
      draws itself properly. The cost is that stroke width is stretched with the
      viewBox, which is why it is written in user units below.
    -->
    <svg class="trace" viewBox="0 0 100 100" preserveAspectRatio="none" aria-hidden="true">
      <!-- (50 + --tx, 50 + --ty) of the sheet each end sits on. Nothing else. -->
      <path style="--d: 0.6" pathLength="1" d="M31 47 C31 60, 40 60, 40 72"></path>
      <path style="--d: 0.66" pathLength="1" d="M46.8 47 C46.8 58, 40 62, 40 72"></path>
      <path style="--d: 0.72" pathLength="1" d="M66 47 C66 58, 60 62, 60 72"></path>
    </svg>

    <!-- The desk. Everything here is cropped by the frame on purpose. -->
    <div class="spread" data-pointer>
      {
        noise.map(({ doc, z }) => (
          <div class={`slot slot--noise slot--${doc.id}`} style={`z-index:${z}`}>
            <div class="drift">
              <div class="float">
                <DocFace doc={doc} variant="display" preview />
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        ))
      }
      {
        sheets.map(({ doc, z }) => (
          <div class={`slot slot--${doc.id}`} style={`z-index:${z}`}>
            <div class="drift">
              <div class="float">
                <DocFace doc={doc} variant="display" />
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </div>

    <!-- The clearing. -->
    <div class="opening__copy frame">
      <div class="say say--hero" data-vis="0 0.15">
        <h1 class="say__headline">
          <span class="say__line-a">{firm.discipline}</span>
          <span class="say__line-b">
            for {firm.audience.clientType} in {firm.audience.homeState}.
          </span>
        </h1>

        <p class="say__promise" set:html={em(hero.promise)} />

        <div class="say__act">
          <span class="say__cta">
            <svg class="say__arrow" viewBox="0 0 88 66" aria-hidden="true">
              <path pathLength="1" d="M5 5c1.5 20 8.5 35.5 22.5 44.5C37 55.5 50 58.5 66 59"></path>
              <path pathLength="1" d="M53 51c5 2.5 9.5 5 13 8-4.5 1.5-8.5 3.5-12 6.5"></path>
            </svg>
            <a class="stamp" href={href(firm.contact.calendarUrl)}>
              <span class="stamp__label">{hero.cta}</span>
            </a>
          </span>
          <span class="say__hint">{hero.ctaHint}</span>
        </div>

        <ul class="say__proof">
          {hero.chips.map((chip) => <li class="say__chip">{chip}</li>)}
        </ul>

        <p class="say__cue" aria-hidden="true">
          <span class="say__cue-rule"></span>
          <span class="say__cue-text u-caps">{hero.scrollCue}</span>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <BeatBand
      beats={[
        { ...beats.arrive, from: 0.08, to: 0.46 },
        { ...beats.sort, from: 0.45 },
      ]}
    />

    <!-- What the heap is worth, before anybody has checked it. -->
    <div class="tally" data-vis="0.11 0.48" aria-hidden="true">
      <p
        class="tally__figure u-num"
        data-count-to={beats.arrive.tally}
        data-count-at="0.17 0.37"
        data-count-fmt="usd0"
      >
        $0
      </p>
      <p class="tally__line">
        {beats.arrive.tallyLabel} · <em>{beats.arrive.tallyNote}</em>
      </p>
    </div>

    <!--
      The honesty label. Every demo of this template carries the same fictional
      March, and this line is what makes a worked example a worked example
      rather than a claim about somebody's results.

      It is a SIBLING of the tally, not a third line inside it. Inside, it made
      the block twenty pixels taller and the paper immediately started landing
      on it at 1024 and above — the guard boxes in the sweep are drawn round
      whatever the visitor is reading, and the tally had quietly grown into the
      paper's airspace. Out here it costs the composition nothing, and it shares
      the foot of the frame with `.onward`, which is never up at the same time.
    -->
    <p class="slug u-caps" data-vis="0.1 0.5" aria-hidden="true">{hero.pileCaption}</p>

    <!-- Hands the visitor the next question at the moment this one resolves. -->
    <p class="onward u-caps" data-vis="0.84 1" aria-hidden="true">{beats.sort.next}</p>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .opening {
    /*
     * One viewport of pin plus the scroll the whole journey is allowed to take.
     * The last sheet is laid out at 83% of that; the rest is the pause the brief
     * asks for — a beat that resolves and then holds reads as confident.
     *
     * `--run` is printed from scenes.ts, which is also what the corner strip
     * divides by. The narrow override below gives this scene ten more vh and is
     * deliberately NOT fed back into that arithmetic: it moves the strip's
     * states by under three per cent, which is inside their own cross-fade.
     */
    height: calc(100svh + var(--run) * 1vh);
  }

  .opening__pin {
    position: sticky;
    top: 0;
    height: 100svh;
    padding-top: var(--mast-h);
    overflow: clip;

    /*
     * One foot line for the three things that live at the bottom of this frame.
     * They used to carry three similar-but-different clamps, which is how the
     * tally and the slug ended up printing on the same row of pixels: nothing
     * in the code said they shared an edge, so nothing stopped them.
     */
    --foot: clamp(0.75rem, 3vh, 2rem);
    --tally-lift: 0.5rem;
  }

  /* ── the clearing ─────────────────────────────────────────────────── */

  .opening__copy {
    position: relative;
    z-index: 2;
    display: grid;
    place-items: center;
    height: 100%;
    padding-bottom: clamp(0.5rem, 2vh, 2rem);
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  .say--hero {
    /* Held to a constant fraction of the viewport rather than a constant number
       of pixels, because the ring outside it is measured in the same units — the
       clearing and the paper around it have to agree at every width. */
    max-width: min(46rem, 56vw);
    text-align: center;
    pointer-events: auto;
    opacity: clamp(0, calc(1 - (var(--p) - 0.015) / 0.11), 1);
    translate: 0 calc(clamp(0, calc((var(--p) - 0.015) / 0.11), 1) * -34px);
  }

  /*
   * TWO VOICES, NOT TWO SIZES.
   *
   * This was one family in one ink at two sizes, and it read as a paragraph
   * somebody had set large — nothing in it told the eye where the sentence
   * turned. The turn is the interesting part: the first line is what the firm
   * does, and the second is WHO IT IS FOR, which is the only clause a rescin
   * ever changes and the only one a visitor checks himself against.
   *
   * So the second clause changes voice as well as size — the serif, in the
   * brand's ink. It is the one place on the first screen where the brand colour
   * lands on a word rather than on a button, which is most of why the screen
   * stopped looking monochrome.
   */
  .say__headline {
    font-weight: 400;
    text-wrap: balance;
  }

  .say__line-a {
    display: block;
    font-size: var(--fs-hero);
    font-weight: 620;
    line-height: 1.02;
    letter-spacing: -0.033em;
    color: var(--desk-ink);
  }

  .say__line-b {
    display: block;
    margin-top: 0.4rem;
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 1.05rem + 1.9vw, 2.375rem);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-style: italic;
    line-height: 1.14;
    letter-spacing: -0.008em;
    color: var(--brand-lit);
  }

  .say__promise {
    margin-inline: auto;
    margin-top: clamp(0.9rem, 2.2vh, 1.4rem);
    max-width: 42ch;
    font-size: var(--fs-lg);
    line-height: 1.42;
    color: var(--desk-ink-quiet);
  }

  .say__act {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    gap: 0.85rem 1.25rem;
    margin-top: clamp(1.25rem, 3.2vh, 2rem);
  }

  .say__cta {
    position: relative;
    display: inline-flex;
  }

  /* The CTA is a stamp, not a rectangle: a double keyline, set slightly off
     square, that presses flat when you push it. */
  .stamp {
    position: relative;
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    padding: 1rem 2.1rem;
    background: var(--brand-solid);
    color: var(--brand-on-solid);
    border-radius: 3px;
    rotate: -1.1deg;
    box-shadow: var(--shadow-rest);
    transition:
      rotate var(--t-base) var(--ease-paper),
      translate var(--t-fast) var(--ease-snap),
      box-shadow var(--t-fast) var(--ease-snap);
  }

  .stamp::before {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    inset: 4px;
    border: 1px solid color-mix(in oklab, var(--brand-on-solid) 42%, transparent);
    border-radius: 1px;
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  .stamp__label {
    font-size: var(--fs-base);
    font-weight: 580;
    letter-spacing: -0.004em;
  }

  /*
   * THE ONLY THING ON THIS PAGE THAT ASKS FOR ATTENTION ON A LOOP.
   *
   * A rubber stamp does not glow and it does not bounce. What it does is press
   * — and the mark of a press is the ring of ink that spreads out from under it
   * and dies. So the button gives off one ring every four and a half seconds,
   * from its own edge, in its own colour, and nothing else moves.
   *
   * It is a pseudo-element and not the button, which matters: an `animation` on
   * `translate` would outrank the `:hover` translate in the cascade and the
   * button would refuse to lift under the cursor. And it stops the moment
   * somebody hovers or tabs to it — a control that keeps waving after you have
   * found it is a control that does not know you are there.
   */
  .stamp::after {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    inset: -2px;
    border: 2px solid var(--brand-solid);
    border-radius: 5px;
    opacity: 0;
    pointer-events: none;
    animation: stamp-halo 4.4s var(--ease-paper) 2.2s infinite;
  }

  @keyframes stamp-halo {
    0% {
      scale: 1 1;
      opacity: 0.5;
    }
    58%,
    100% {
      scale: 1.11 1.34;
      opacity: 0;
    }
  }

  .stamp:hover::after,
  .stamp:focus-visible::after {
    animation: none;
  }

  .stamp:hover {
    rotate: 0deg;
    translate: 0 -2px;
    box-shadow: var(--shadow-lift);
  }

  .stamp:active {
    translate: 0 1px;
    box-shadow: var(--shadow-flat);
  }

  /* Somebody drew this in on the printout. It arrives a beat after the page has
     settled, so it reads as a mark made rather than an element that loaded. */
  .say__arrow {
    position: absolute;
    right: calc(100% + 0.35rem);
    bottom: 0.55rem;
    width: 5.5rem;
    height: auto;
    fill: none;
    stroke: var(--desk-mark);
    stroke-width: 2.1;
    stroke-linecap: round;
    stroke-linejoin: round;
    opacity: 0.85;
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  .say__arrow path {
    stroke-dasharray: 1;
    stroke-dashoffset: 1;
    animation: draw-in 620ms var(--ease-seek) forwards;
  }

  .say__arrow path:first-child {
    animation-delay: 1.15s;
  }

  .say__arrow path:last-child {
    animation-delay: 1.62s;
  }

  @keyframes draw-in {
    to {
      stroke-dashoffset: 0;
    }
  }

  .say__hint {
    font-size: var(--fs-sm);
    color: var(--desk-ink-quiet);
  }

  .say__proof {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    justify-content: center;
    gap: 0.45rem 0.55rem;
    margin-top: clamp(1rem, 2.6vh, 1.6rem);
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  /*
   * Three facts, as three objects.
   *
   * They were three lines of grey mono with a tick in front, floating on bare
   * desk — which is how a caption looks, not how proof looks. On a desk made of
   * paper the way to make something matter is to give it paper: a small ticket,
   * lying slightly out of true, catching the same light as everything else.
   */
  .say__chip {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.4rem;
    padding: 0.34rem 0.7rem 0.36rem;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--fs-xs);
    letter-spacing: -0.012em;
    color: var(--paper-ink);
    background: var(--paper);
    border-radius: 2px;
    white-space: nowrap;
    box-shadow:
      inset 0 0 0 1px var(--paper-rule),
      var(--shadow-flat);
  }

  .say__chip::before {
    content: '✓';
    font-size: 0.95em;
    line-height: 1;
    color: var(--brand-ink);
  }

  /* Nothing on a desk is square to the desk. */
  .say__chip:nth-child(odd) {
    rotate: -0.5deg;
  }

  .say__chip:nth-child(even) {
    rotate: 0.6deg;
  }

  .say__cue {
    display: grid;
    justify-items: center;
    gap: 0.45rem;
    margin-top: clamp(1.4rem, 4.5vh, 3rem);
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  .say__cue-rule {
    width: 1px;
    height: clamp(1.25rem, 3.4vh, 2.25rem);
    background: linear-gradient(var(--desk-mark), transparent);
    transform-origin: top;
    animation: cue-drop 2.8s var(--ease-seek) infinite;
  }

  @keyframes cue-drop {
    0%,
    62%,
    100% {
      scale: 1 1;
      opacity: 0.8;
    }
    31% {
      scale: 1 0.42;
      opacity: 1;
    }
  }

  .say__cue-text {
    font-size: var(--fs-2xs);
    letter-spacing: 0.16em;
    color: var(--desk-mark);
  }

  /* ── what the heap is worth ───────────────────────────────────────── */

  .tally {
    position: absolute;
    left: 50%;
    bottom: calc(var(--foot) + var(--tally-lift));
    z-index: 2;
    width: min(40rem, calc(100% - 2 * var(--gutter)));
    text-align: center;
    pointer-events: none;
    --in: min(
      clamp(0, calc((var(--p) - 0.14) / 0.05), 1),
      clamp(0, calc((0.46 - var(--p)) / 0.05), 1)
    );
    opacity: var(--in);
    translate: -50% calc((1 - var(--in)) * 16px);
  }

  .tally__figure {
    font-size: clamp(2.25rem, 1.4rem + 3.4vw, 4.25rem);
    font-weight: 450;
    line-height: 1;
    letter-spacing: -0.045em;
    color: var(--desk-ink);
  }

  .tally__line {
    margin-top: 0.4rem;
    font-size: var(--fs-base);
    line-height: 1.35;
    color: var(--desk-ink-quiet);
    text-wrap: balance;
  }

  .tally__line em {
    font-style: normal;
    color: var(--desk-ink);
  }

  /*
   * A printer's slug, in the corner of the proof.
   *
   * It used to be centred at the foot of the frame — at the same `bottom` as
   * the tally, which is a block with a line of copy at ITS foot. The two
   * printed straight through each other at 900px tall: "moved through the
   * business in March" and "Illustrative figures · March · USD" occupying one
   * line of pixels. Two things anchored to the same edge of the same box will
   * always find each other; the fix is not more clearance, it is a different
   * corner. Bottom right is empty desk in every state of this scene, and the
   * bottom left already belongs to the running strip.
   */
  .slug {
    position: absolute;
    right: var(--gutter);
    bottom: var(--foot);
    z-index: 2;
    font-size: var(--fs-2xs);
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    color: var(--desk-mark);
    white-space: nowrap;
    pointer-events: none;
    opacity: min(
      clamp(0, calc((var(--p) - 0.14) / 0.05), 1),
      clamp(0, calc((0.46 - var(--p)) / 0.05), 1)
    );
  }

  /* ── the next question ────────────────────────────────────────────── */

  .onward {
    position: absolute;
    left: 50%;
    bottom: var(--foot);
    z-index: 2;
    font-size: var(--fs-2xs);
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    color: var(--desk-mark);
    white-space: nowrap;
    translate: -50% 0;
    pointer-events: none;
    opacity: clamp(0, calc((var(--p) - 0.85) / 0.06), 1);
  }

  /* ── the paper ────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */

  /* Covers the whole frame including the strip behind the letterhead, so paper
     can run under the header the way it would on a real desk. */
  .spread {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    z-index: 1;
    container-type: size;
    pointer-events: none;
    user-select: none;
  }

  .slot {
    /*
     * Width is capped in BOTH axes: --w hundredths of the frame's width, and
     * --wh hundredths of its height. A document is about 0.9 as tall as it is
     * wide, so the second cap fixes the sheet's vertical reach whatever the
     * viewport's proportions are.
     *
     * That is the whole reason the clearing stays clear — "does this sheet
     * touch the headline" becomes arithmetic instead of something you discover
     * at 1100px — and it is what lets one set of numbers survive a 390×844
     * phone and an 860×844 tablet: on both the sheet is the same fraction of
     * the frame's HEIGHT, which is the axis this scene fights over.
     *
     * The default ties them at 1.72 — the ratio at which a 16:9 frame makes both
     * caps agree — so a scene that does not care only sets --w.
     */
    --wh: calc(var(--w) * 1.72);
    --sw: min(var(--w) * 1cqw, var(--wh) * 1cqh);
    --ms: 1;
    --ts: var(--ms);
    --wx: var(--ptr-x2);
    --wy: var(--ptr-y2);

    /*
     * Two knobs for short viewports, so a 700-tall laptop or an older phone is
     * corrected in one place rather than by editing nine sets of coordinates.
     */
    --msk: 1;
    --cyk: 0;
    --cyr: calc(var(--cy) + var(--cyk));
    --msf: calc(var(--ms) * var(--msk));
    --tsf: calc(var(--ts) * var(--msk));

    /*
     * Segment 3, the hand-off: once the month is sorted, two of the five are
     * picked up to be checked against each other and the other three are set
     * aside. It rides on .drift rather than on the main expression because by
     * then the wind is dead and that wrapper is free — and because a third term
     * inside the main translate would make it unreadable.
     */
    --hx: 0;
    --hy: 0;
    --hf: clamp(0, calc((var(--p) - 0.84) / 0.13), 1);
    --he: calc(var(--hf) * var(--hf) * (3 - 2 * var(--hf)));

    /* Segment 1: in from the edges into a heap. Segment 2: sorted and laid out. */
    --f1: clamp(0, calc((var(--p) - var(--d1)) / var(--dur1)), 1);
    --f2: clamp(0, calc((var(--p) - var(--d2)) / var(--dur2)), 1);
    /* Smoothstep: leaves rest gently, arrives gently. Paper does not bounce. */
    --e1: calc(var(--f1) * var(--f1) * (3 - 2 * var(--f1)));
    --e2: calc(var(--f2) * var(--f2) * (3 - 2 * var(--f2)));
    /* Off the desk during either move, flat before and after. */
    --air: max(sin(calc(var(--f1) * 180deg)), sin(calc(var(--f2) * 180deg)));
    /* Loose until it joins the heap — after that the wind stops reaching it. */
    --loose: calc(1 - var(--e1));

    position: absolute;
    left: 50%;
    top: 50%;
    width: var(--sw);

    translate:
      calc(
        -50% +
          (
            var(--cx) + (var(--mx) - var(--cx)) * var(--e1) + (var(--tx) - var(--mx)) *
              var(--e2)
          ) * 1cqw
      )
      calc(
        -50% +
          (
            var(--cyr) + (var(--my) - var(--cyr)) * var(--e1) + (var(--ty) - var(--my)) *
              var(--e2)
          ) * 1cqh
      );

    rotate: calc(
      (
          var(--crot) + (var(--mrot) - var(--crot)) * var(--e1) + (var(--trot) - var(--mrot)) *
            var(--e2) + sin(var(--f1) * 360deg) * var(--wob) + sin(var(--f2) * 180deg) *
            var(--wob)
        ) * 1deg
    );

    /* Higher above the desk reads as slightly nearer the camera. Together with
       the shadow below, this is what makes it a flight rather than a slide. */
    scale: calc(
      (1 + (var(--msf) - 1) * var(--e1) + (var(--tsf) - var(--msf)) * var(--e2)) *
        (1 + var(--air) * 0.075)
    );

    filter: drop-shadow(
      0 calc((3 + var(--air) * 30) * 1px) calc((7 + var(--air) * 30) * 1px)
        rgb(0 0 0 / calc(var(--shade-a) * (0.6 + var(--air) * 0.4)))
    );
  }

  /*
   * The totals are not printed yet.
   *
   * A document in the heap is raw paper. The figure at its foot is the
   * bookkeeper's output, so it is inked in as the sheet is separated out and
   * named — the sorting beat literally puts the numbers on the page. It also
   * keeps the answer off the first screen, which matters most on a narrow one,
   * where a small sheet prints its name and total at the top.
   */
  .slot :global(.doc__total-amount) {
    opacity: var(--e2);
  }

  /*
   * The wind. Each sheet subscribes to one of the three pointer channels the
   * engine publishes; the slow ones are physically further away, so they answer
   * late and by less. --ptr-v is how hard the air is moving, and it tips the
   * sheet as well as sliding it — a gust catches a corner, it does not push a
   * page sideways.
   */
  .drift {
    translate: calc(
        var(--wx) * var(--depth) * var(--loose) * -1px + var(--hx) * var(--he) * 1cqw
      )
      calc(var(--wy) * var(--depth) * var(--loose) * -0.5px + var(--hy) * var(--he) * 1cqh);
    rotate: calc(
      var(--loose) * (var(--wx) * var(--tilt) * -1 + var(--ptr-v) * var(--gustr)) * 1deg
    );
  }

  /*
   * The two that are going on to be checked settle a little further down the
   * desk, towards the marks that lead off the bottom of the frame.
   *
   * They used to slide APART as well, and that had to go: the mark now leaves
   * each sheet's centre and runs dead straight to the frame edge, where the
   * next scene picks it up at the same x. A sheet drifting sideways after its
   * mark is drawn pulls the sheet off the top of its own line — the one thing
   * this routing exists to prevent. They already take the sides they will have
   * next screen, invoiced left and banked right, without moving another inch.
   */
  .slot--invoices,
  .slot--bank {
    --hx: 0;
    --hy: 3;
  }

  /*
   * The sheet's one size. Every dimension inside a document is em of this, so a
   * sheet scales as a single object and no label can ever leave its paper.
   *
   * It is set HERE and not on .slot as a custom property, and that is not a
   * style preference. An unregistered custom property is substituted where it is
   * USED — inside `.doc` — and `.doc` is itself a container query container, so
   * `1cqw` there would mean one percent of the sheet's own width. A font size
   * that depends on the width it is helping to compute is a cycle, the browser
   * drops the declaration at computed-value time, and every document silently
   * renders at the inherited body size. Declared on this wrapper, which is not
   * a container, the units resolve against the desk, as intended.
   */
  .float {
    position: relative;
    font-size: max(8.5px, calc(var(--sw) * 0.05));
    animation: breathe var(--bd) var(--ease-seek) var(--bdelay) infinite alternate;
  }

  @keyframes breathe {
    from {
      translate: calc(var(--loose) * -2.5px) calc(var(--loose) * 1.8px);
      rotate: calc(var(--loose) * -0.5deg);
    }
    to {
      translate: calc(var(--loose) * 3px) calc(var(--loose) * -2.4px);
      rotate: calc(var(--loose) * 0.55deg);
    }
  }

  /*
   * The clutter. Never readable, never counted — it is the volume the five real
   * documents get pulled out of, and five sheets spaced around a 1600px frame
   * are not a volume. It slides off the desk before the real paper heaps up.
   */
  .slot--noise {
    --d1: 0.015;
    --dur1: 0.13;
    --d2: 9;
    --dur2: 1;
    --wob: 0;
    opacity: calc(1 - var(--e1));
  }

  /* ── the pencil ───────────────────────────────────────────────────── */

  .trace {
    position: absolute;
    /*
     * `inset: 0` resolves against the pin's PADDING box, so this canvas is the
     * whole 100svh frame — not the area below the letterhead. The next scene's
     * canvas is set up the same way and its top edge is this one's bottom edge,
     * which is what lets a vertical at 37.5% run through the join without a
     * kink. Stroke width, colour and opacity are matched to it exactly for the
     * same reason: a 15% step in either reads as two different lines.
     */
    inset: 0;
    /* Under the paper. Both ends of every mark are under a sheet, so nothing
       can be seen to stop short of the thing it points at. */
    z-index: 1;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    fill: none;
    stroke: var(--desk-ink-quiet);
    /* User units, not pixels: the viewBox is stretched, so this is multiplied
       by the horizontal scale. Across the widths that show these lines at all
       (901px and up) it lands between 1.4 and 2.9 device pixels. */
    stroke-width: 0.15;
    stroke-linecap: round;
    /* Out before the scene lets go. Nothing pencilled here may still be on
       screen when the next one arrives. */
    opacity: calc(0.5 * clamp(0, calc((0.97 - var(--p)) / 0.07), 1));
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  /*
   * Drawn as the sheet it LEAVES lands — which is why the two records are
   * sorted before the three cost documents below. A mark drawn towards a sheet
   * that has not arrived yet ends on bare desk, and that is the whole fault
   * this routing exists to remove.
   */
  .trace path {
    --f: clamp(0, calc((var(--p) - var(--d)) / var(--dur, 0.17)), 1);
    stroke-dasharray: 1;
    stroke-dashoffset: calc(1 - var(--f) * var(--f) * (3 - 2 * var(--f)));
  }

  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    /* The narrow layout sorts into a column that already runs downwards; five
       more lines saying so would be noise. */
    .trace {
      display: none;
    }
  }

  /* ── the ring, the heap, the sorted grid ──────────────────────────── */

  /*
   * Rest (--cx,--cy) is a point in hundredths of the frame from its centre, so
   * ±50 is the edge. A sheet is cropped when its own half width or half height
   * carries it past ±50, and it clears the clearing when one of its two axes
   * stays outside it.
   *
   * A sheet cropped by the LEFT edge shows its right-hand side, which is the
   * column the amounts are set in — so the left edge belongs to paper with no
   * amounts on it, and any real document over there is cropped by the bottom as
   * well, so its foot goes off-frame.
   */

  /*
   * THE TWO SIZES THE FIVE REAL DOCUMENTS SHARE.
   *
   * They were repeated inside each of the five, which made "make the paper
   * smaller" a five-place edit and guaranteed that one of them would eventually
   * be missed. They are one number each now, and they are the number that
   * changed most in this pass.
   *
   * The laid-out size went from 0.78 to 0.62 — a fifth off each edge, a third
   * off the area. The scene was reading as an animation with a caption: five
   * documents filling two thirds of the screen under a heading barely larger
   * than body copy. The sentence is the argument and the paper illustrates it,
   * so the type went up 40% and the paper came down to pay for it.
   *
   * The heap is cut by less. It is the one moment in the scene where volume IS
   * the point — a month arriving as too much paper — and shrinking it as far as
   * the sorted grid would have argued the opposite of what it is there to say.
   */
  .slot--operating,
  .slot--tax,
  .slot--payroll,
  .slot--bank,
  .slot--invoices {
    --ms: 0.94;
    --ts: 0.62;
  }

  .slot--operating {
    --w: 19;
    --cx: -48;
    --cy: 42;
    --crot: 7;
    --mx: -11;
    --my: 12;
    --mrot: -13;
